---
/**
 * foodcraft / FooterBO — "Sipsip Tea Bar" · THE CLOSING BLUSH PAGE.
 *
 * ROLE: the last place a visitor looks for the name, the number, the address
 * and the way back into the page before they give up. So every cell here is
 * real owner data, or the cell is not drawn.
 *
 * WHAT THE DESIGN DRAWS (reproduced value for value from the element carrying
 * data-component="FOOTER" in "Sipsip Tea Bar Landing.dc.html" — nothing
 * re-derived, nothing rounded): no band and no background of its own, so the
 * blush --paper ground simply continues; 56px of air above and 40px below; a
 * minmax(0,1.5fr) / minmax(0,1fr) / minmax(0,1fr) grid at a 34px gap holding
 * the brand lock (a 36px purple disc carrying the shop's initial beside a 24px
 * Baloo 2 wordmark, 10px apart, 13px of space under them), a 14px/1.78 blurb
 * capped at 34ch, and one 13.5px purple action; then two link columns, each
 * opened by a 10px purple caps heading at .24em and 13px of space, over 14px
 * rows on a ~27px pitch. The grid is CLOSED by a hairline 32px under it, and
 * 20px below that a two-cell colophon of 11px/600 --muted text, space-between.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string in this file, and nothing else. Each
 * is declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts, so each is a hook a
 * sidebar input actually owns (a data-field on any other path is a dead hook):
 *   footer.brand                     — the 24px wordmark, and the colophon's
 *                                      identity half (HeaderBO's rail wordmark
 *                                      is the same field: one box, three places)
 *   footer.blurb                     — the standing line under the lock
 *   footer.cta.text                       — the purple action's label (see D3)
 *   footer.cta.href                       — its target (data-href-field)
 *   footer.explore.title             — the LINKS column heading
 *   footer.explore.links.${i}.text   — a links-column label, when the owner
 *   footer.explore.links.${i}.href     filled the footer's own links list
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label          — a links-column label, when the column is
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href             mirroring the rail instead (see D2)
 *   footer.visit.title               — the CONTACT column heading
 *   location.address                 — the address line, and the colophon's
 *     or contact.address               other half — whichever box holds it (D5)
 *   location.phone                   — the tel: row (data-field only, D6)
 *     or contact.phone
 *   contact.email                    — the mailto: row (D7)
 *   footer.visit.lines.${i}          — the contact column's extra static rows,
 *                                      which is what stands where the design
 *                                      drew "Hours & directions" (D8)
 *   footer.notes.${i}                — the colophon's RIGHT cell (D1)
 *
 * DERIVED here, never authored here:
 *   · THE DISC'S LETTER. The design's circle holds `{{ initial }}`. It is the
 *     first character of the wordmark the owner supplied, upper-cased, and it
 *     leaves the moment the wordmark does. Array.from() rather than charAt() so
 *     a name opening on an astral character yields that whole character and not
 *     half a surrogate pair. It carries no hook of its own — it is aria-hidden
 *     decoration whose source is the field two nodes away — which does mean a
 *     LIVE inline edit of the wordmark leaves the letter stale until the next
 *     build. Hooking it would offer an editor a one-character box that writes
 *     into the whole name, which is worse.
 *   · the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when footer.brand is
 *     blank — the same name out of the same submission, not an invention.
 *   · the blurb falls back to footer.brand_blurb, the schema's own alias.
 *   · the tel: href is built from the phone's own digits and the mailto: href
 *     from the email. Neither prefix is ever stored (D6).
 *   · every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBO really
 *     renders. PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS are byte-identical to HeaderBO's,
 *     so the rail and the footer can never send a visitor to two places.
 *   · `i` on every list row is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter index (R6), so dropping
 *     an unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3. The colophon's
 *     separators are drawn from the DISPLAY position instead, since they are
 *     decoration and nothing edits them.
 *   · the colophon's address is FLATTENED to one line (newlines to commas) to
 *     fit a single 11px row — which is why that node, and only that node,
 *     carries data-derived: editorBridge's ed:update would otherwise paint the
 *     raw multi-line scalar into a one-line row. The COLUMN's address is NOT
 *     derived: it is the stored string verbatim, broken by `white-space:
 *     pre-line`, so an inline edit there stays truthful.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. "Sipsip", "Menu", "The drinks", "Our story",
 *     "FAQ", "Find us", "Hours & directions", "Contact us", the blurb, the
 *     address and "Prices in Philippine pesos" are the DESIGNER filling in
 *     their own template. Not one of those words is in this file. A tea bar
 *     that supplied no links gets no links column, not the designer's three.
 *   · a column heading with no column under it, a lock with no name, a blurb
 *     with no text, a link with no label or no reachable target, an action with
 *     no label, a colophon with nothing in it, and the whole footer when all of
 *     the above are empty. EVERY guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so a blank value
 *     removes the ELEMENT and not just its text.
 *   · THE GRID'S CLOSING HAIRLINE when nothing follows it. The design draws
 *     that rule BETWEEN the grid and the colophon, so it rides a modifier
 *     (.sip-fgrid--ruled) the markup adds only when the colophon renders.
 *     Otherwise a site with a phone but no name and no address would close on a
 *     lilac hairline floating 40px above the bottom of the page (R2: no
 *     hairline under a row that is not there).
 *   · THE STICKY ORDER BAR the design draws immediately AFTER the footer. It is
 *     not inside data-component="FOOTER" and carries no data-component of its
 *     own, so it belongs to no section in this build; its one string,
 *     `barLine` ("Order ahead — ready in about 10 minutes"), is a turnaround-
 *     time promise with no schema field behind it; and a fixed bar with no
 *     dismiss would sit over every screen of a page whose CtaBand already draws
 *     the same two actions. FLAG FOR REVIEW: if that bar is wanted, it is a
 *     component of its own with a field of its own, not a tail on this file.
 *   · footer.locality. The rail prints the town tag because the design's
 *     HEADER draws one; the design's FOOTER lock is disc plus name and nothing
 *     else, so repeating the tag here would be composing a lock the design
 *     does not have.
 *   · footer.hours and footer.social. Both are declared in this very schema
 *     group, and this design's footer draws neither an hours table nor a social
 *     row. LocationBO owns the hours.
 *   · .reveal. The footer is the page terminus; fading in the last thing on
 *     screen buys nothing and risks a footer that never un-hides.
 *   · any star row, rating or numeral. R2's star rule has nothing to bite on
 *     here — there is not one node on this band that could print a claim the
 *     owner did not make.
 *   · role="img" and .plate (R9). There is no upload anywhere in this footer.
 *     The one graphic is the initial disc, which is decoration over text that
 *     is already announced beside it, so it is aria-hidden and so are the
 *     colophon separators.
 *
 * DECISIONS a reader would otherwise take for an oversight
 *
 * D1. THE COLOPHON'S RIGHT CELL IS footer.notes, NOT THE CURRENCY SENTENCE.
 *     The design prints `Prices in {{ currencyName }}` there. That is a claim
 *     about how this business quotes and no field carries it, so the sentence
 *     is gone — but the CELL is not, because footer.notes is declared in this
 *     group, labelled "Footer notes (copyright row)", and
 *     lib/derive-content-defaults.ts seeds it to `© <year> <name>` from the
 *     owner's own submitted name (and deliberately suppresses a bare "© 2026"
 *     with no name). So the design's two-cell row survives with the owner's own
 *     small print in it. A note that repeats the left cell VERBATIM is dropped
 *     — an exact-match test only, because "© 2026 Sipsip" is different
 *     information from "Sipsip · 24 Esteban Abada St" and both belong there.
 *     This follows FooterBM and FooterBN; FooterBL dropped the cell instead.
 *
 * D2. THE LINKS COLUMN HAS THREE SOURCES AND ONLY TWO HOOK SHAPES.
 *     footer.explore.links is the schema's own list for this column, so it wins
 *     when the owner filled it. Otherwise the column mirrors the rail, reading
 *     content.navbar_links — the same array HeaderBO reads FIRST, so the two
 *     can never disagree — and hooks `navbar_links.${i}.*`. The third case is
 *     the one to notice: lib/astro-builder.ts substitutes its OWN four links
 *     ("About / Services / Gallery / Contact") onto layout.navLinks when
 *     content.navbar_links is absent entirely. Those labels are nobody's words,
 *     so those rows render with NO hook rather than pointing an editor at a
 *     path that holds nothing. Astro omits an undefined attribute, so this
 *     costs no markup.
 *
 * D3. THE ACTION IS COUNTED BY THE BRAND COLUMN'S OWN GATE. The design draws
 *     contactLabel exactly once in this footer, in the brand column, so unlike
 *     FooterBM there is no second place for it to fall to and no stutter to
 *     guard against. It therefore joins brand and blurb in deciding whether
 *     that column exists at all: with no name and no blurb but a real action,
 *     dropping the column would throw away a working link rather than avoid an
 *     empty one. That does not breach R5 — a CTA is body content with a real
 *     target, not a tag and not a headline.
 *     The pair hooked is footer.cta.text / footer.cta.href, this section's own, not
 *     hero.cta1: components/editor/editorBridge.ts resolves a Blocks toggle by
 *     counting hooks of a given prefix inside closest('section,footer'), so a
 *     footer carrying hero.* hooks would become a candidate for "hide the Hero
 *     block". A footer.* hook belongs to the FOOTER block instead, whose own
 *     toggle already resolves to this component, so the pair is still
 *     toggle-safe. Note HeaderBO deliberately does NOT bind this pair (its
 *     design has no rail button), so on this template the footer is the only
 *     place the sidebar's "Header CTA" box reaches a pixel.
 *
 * D4. THE COLUMN HEADINGS ARE NOT .kick, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. The wrapper's
 *     .kick is 11px at .18em; the design's footer headings are 10px at .24em.
 *     Those are different metrics, not a near-miss, so the heading is a scoped
 *     class at the design's own numbers, taking the same --accent that makes it
 *     read as part of this family. They stay <h4> because two real headings
 *     give the columns a structure a screen-reader user can navigate; the
 *     design's plain <div> does not. (A scoped class has to restate them AND
 *     out-specify them: `html[data-page="BO"] :where(h1,h2,h3,h4)` scores
 *     (0,1,1) — the `html` type selector counts — and pins family, weight,
 *     colour, tracking, uppercasing, line-height and margin, while an
 *     unqualified scoped class scores only (0,1,0), because Astro emits its
 *     scope as `:where(.astro-<hash>)` and :where() adds nothing. See S1 in the
 *     stylesheet: every rule in this file that contests a wrapper rule is
 *     qualified by its parent class for exactly that reason.)
 *
 * D5. THE ADDRESS AND THE PHONE HOOK WHICHEVER BOX ACTUALLY HOLDS THEM. The
 *     schema declares the same two facts twice: location.address /
 *     location.phone in the Location group, and contact.address / contact.phone
 *     in the Footer and Header groups. Both are live inputs, so the hook
 *     follows the source rather than being pinned to one path — an inline edit
 *     then writes back into the box the value came out of instead of forking a
 *     second copy that wins on the next build. Location is read first because
 *     LocationBO's own doc block states that this file is what keeps
 *     location.address editable (its headline IS the address but hooks
 *     location.headline).
 *     ONE consequence to know about: `location` IS a prefix in editorBridge's
 *     Blocks map, so these hooks make this footer a candidate host for "hide
 *     the Location block". That resolver scores by COUNT and LocationBO carries
 *     more location.* hooks than the two here, so the real section always wins.
 *   · WHY THE contact.phone BRANCH IS data-derived: astro-builder passes
 *     content.contact through with `phone: formatPhoneDisplay(...)`, so that
 *     branch prints a display string that is not byte-identical to the stored
 *     one. data-derived keeps ed:update from painting the raw scalar into a row
 *     that shows the formatted one; click-to-focus still works. The
 *     location.phone branch and content.location generally pass through
 *     untouched, so they need no such opt-out.
 *
 * D6. THE tel: ANCHOR CARRIES data-field ONLY, no data-href-field. Its href is
 *     derived from the field's own value, so a href hook there would write
 *     "tel:+63…" straight back into the phone box. Same for the mailto: row.
 *
 * D7. THE EMAIL ROW IS AN ADDITION. The design draws none — its demo bar took
 *     orders on Messenger — but contact.email is schema-declared in the very
 *     group this footer belongs to and is bound by no other BO section, so a
 *     real business that supplies one had it appear NOWHERE on the page. It
 *     takes the design's own 14px row treatment, so nothing new is invented
 *     visually.
 *
 * D8. footer.visit.lines STANDS WHERE "Hours & directions" WAS. The design's
 *     third contact row is an in-page link whose LABEL is the designer's words,
 *     and no field carries a label for it, so the link is gone. The slot is
 *     not: "Visit column lines" is a declared list for exactly this column, so
 *     an owner who wants a third line there has one, in their own words.
 *
 * D9. PHONE AND EMAIL ARE UNDERLINED, THE STATIC LINES ARE NOT. In this design
 *     the contact column's address and its phone link are the SAME family, size
 *     and colour — the design gives the link no distinguishing treatment at all
 *     — so without an underline nothing but hover would mark it as a link
 *     (WCAG 1.4.1). text-decoration adds no box, so thickening it on hover
 *     cannot shift the rows. Deliberately NOT applied to the links column:
 *     those are wayfinding links in a list, unambiguous by position and
 *     heading, and the design draws them bare.
 *
 * D10. NO .btn IN THIS FILE, AT ALL (R4). lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts emits
 *      `.testi .btn, .cta-band .btn, footer .btn { color: <on-ink> !important }`
 *      — a .btn anywhere under this <footer> would take a label colour picked
 *      for an INK ground and vanish on this blush one. The design's footer has
 *      no button anyway (a 13.5px text action and 14px rows), so the action is
 *      the wrapper's .linkc and the rows are plain anchors, neither of which
 *      the engine touches. None of .btn--fill / .btn--pink / .btn--cream /
 *      .btn--line appears, and neither does any forbidden name.
 *
 * D11. THE COLOPHON IS NOT .mono. The wrapper's .mono is 11px uppercase at
 *      .16em and weight 700; this design's colophon is 11px weight 600, mixed
 *      case, NO tracking. Borrowing .mono would shout a row the design speaks,
 *      so every declaration is restated at the design's own numbers. Its colour
 *      is --muted, which is the design's #6B6067 exactly.
 *
 * D12. NO TOP HAIRLINE AND NO BAND. Every other seam in this design is a rule
 *      or a colour change, so their absence here looks like an omission. It is
 *      the design: the footer is bare --paper continuing straight out of the
 *      section above, whose CTA is a purple plate inset from the page edge.
 *      A --line rule across the top would draw a lilac hairline the design
 *      never had, and .band-pink / .band-sage / .band-lilac are all wrong here
 *      for the same reason — the design puts no ground under this footer.
 *
 * D13. THE ROWS ARE BLOCKS, NOT THE DESIGN'S <p>. The design uses
 *      `<p style="margin:0 0 8px">` per row over a body with no declared
 *      line-height — about a 27px pitch. Here each row is a block at 14px/1.5
 *      with 3px of padding either side: the same ~27px pitch, the whole row is
 *      the tap target, and no trailing margin dangles above the hairline.
 *
 * COLOUR — wrapper tokens only, no literal hex anywhere (R1). Every colour this
 * design's footer uses has a token holding the same value:
 *   #FFF7F2 --paper (the ground) and --on-accent (the disc letter)
 *   #241A22 --ink (wordmark) · #7B4FA8 --accent (disc, headings, action)
 *   #5D5158 --ink-soft (blurb and every row) · #6B6067 --muted (the colophon)
 *   #5C3580 --accent-deep (the design's own stated hover: its stylesheet
 *   declares a:hover{color:#5C3580}, shadowed in the demo only by the inline
 *   colour on each footer link).
 *   THE ONE VALUE WITH NO EXACT TOKEN is the hairline, #EFE6EB. PageBO declares
 *   --line as #EEE2F5 (the same value as --lilac and --well), so the hairline
 *   role token is a hair cooler than the design's warm grey. --line ships,
 *   because the alternative is a literal hex the theme engine cannot repaint.
 *   Flagged rather than quietly absorbed. Nothing else needed re-deriving.
 *
 * CONTRAST — every run in this file sits on --paper, so the figures are PageBO's
 * own verified table, not new arithmetic: ink 15.92 · ink-soft 7.13 · muted
 * 5.68 · accent 5.63. The disc letter is --on-accent on an --accent fill, which
 * that table gives as 5.63. HeaderBO's table gives --accent-deep on --paper as
 * 8.72, which is the hover state here. Nothing on this band is below AA. The
 * pair this palette fails on — --accent on --pink at 3.93 — cannot occur here:
 * there is no pink in this footer.
 *
 * RESPONSIVE (R8): the wrapper owns the shared 760 / 520 pass; this file owns
 * its own grid. Three columns cannot hold 14px rows and a 34ch blurb below
 * ~900px, so they stack there at ONE width — there is never a band where a
 * column is 90px wide. There is NO clamp() in this file: every size is a fixed
 * px that only ever steps DOWN as the viewport narrows (padding 56/40 → 40/28,
 * gap 34 → 28 → 24, hairline inset 32 → 26 → 22, disc 36 → 32, disc letter
 * 20 → 18, wordmark 24 → 21 at a constant 1.1 line-height, heading tracking
 * .24em → .18em, colophon gap 16 → 10). No floor in the 900 query sits above a
 * cap in the 520 query, and the row type holds the design's 14px at every width.
 * The only two values that grow are touch targets, not type or plates: at 900 a
 * link row takes a 44px floor and a static line takes 9px of padding, because
 * that is where the columns stack and a row becomes a primary target.
 * Long unbroken strings get overflow-wrap: anywhere on every text node; the
 * action additionally declares white-space: normal so a label with no spaces in
 * it wraps instead of escaping.
 *
 * CLASS NAMES are `sip-f*`. `sip-` is this template's prefix throughout — the
 * template briefly shipped three (`sip-` on eleven sections, `ss-` on Hero and
 * CtaBand, `ssbo-` on Header) and was unified on this one — and no
 * name in this file is used by any other BO section — the nearest neighbours are
 * FaqBO's `sip-faq*`, which share the stem and collide with nothing (the
 * stylesheet is scoped either way). Nothing here shares a name with FooterBL's
 * `fb-`, FooterBM's `tcbm-` or FooterBN's `pcbn-` set — an audit had to rename
 * a whole set of classes copied verbatim between templates in this family.
 *
 * Visibility: PageBO gates this file on visibility.footerSection — never here.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const c = (content as any) || {};
const lay = (layout as any) || {};
const f = c.footer || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD (R7) — byte-identical to HeaderBO's, so the rail and the
// footer can never send a visitor to different places.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes nav links to '#about', '#services',
// '#why', '#work', '#visit' and navCtaHref to '#visit'; lib/astro-builder.ts
// falls back to '#about', '#services', '#gallery', '#contact'. Most of those
// ids no design renders. Resolve every in-page href against what PageBO
// ACTUALLY renders: keep it if the id exists, remap it when this design carries
// the same material under another name, otherwise fall back — and the links
// column passes '' as the fallback, because a dead entry in a wayfinding list is
// worse than a missing one. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http(s), anything
// without a leading '#') pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames five derived targets: the services block IS the drinks
// menu (rooms lands there too), '#about' is the shop's own story (host and why
// are the same block here), '#work' is the photo wall, and '#location' /
// '#contact' both mean the visit block — address, map and opening hours. A
// booking link has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'visit',
  host: 'about',
  location: 'visit',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. `||`, never `??`, and identical to HeaderBO's resolution:
// genericContentSchema gives footer.brand fallbackPaths ['business_name'] and
// ContentFieldsAuto's readWithFallbacks treats '' as empty, so a CLEARED brand
// box still shows the business name in the sidebar. With `??` an empty string
// would be a value and the page would go wordmark-less while the sidebar
// displayed a name. 'Your Business' once shipped as a real customer's own mark;
// with nothing supplied the lock is omitted instead of inventing a name.
const brand = String(f.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// The design's disc letter — derived from the name above, never authored, and
// gone the moment the name is.
const initial = (Array.from(brand)[0] || '').toUpperCase();
// footer.brand_blurb is the schema's declared alias for the same box.
const blurb = String(f.blurb || f.brand_blurb || '').trim();

// This section's own action (see the note below). '#visit' is the fallback
// because that is where this design puts the address, the map and the opening
// hours, and it is the id the derived default already names.
// THIS FOOTER'S OWN action. footer.cta.* is read FIRST and falls back to
// navCtaText / navCtaHref — the HEADER's field, which is exactly what these two
// lines read before — so a site that never filled the footer's box in renders
// byte-identically and no live footer loses its link. The hooks below spell
// footer.cta.*, so the owner's first edit lands there and this action's label,
// link and colour part company with the header pill's from then on. That split
// is the whole point: one field used to print "Get in touch" in the top bar and
// again down here, and an owner asked why.
const ctaText = String(f.cta?.text ?? '').trim() || String(c.navCtaText ?? '').trim();
const ctaHref = onPage(f.cta?.href || c.navCtaHref, '#visit');
// Computed, never literal, and ONE conditional per attribute — an owner may
// point this at #visit or at a tel:, and forcing those into a new tab is wrong.
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// Column headings — bound, not hardcoded, and guarded, so an owner who clears
// the box gets a column with no heading rather than the designer's word. The
// schema's own owner-facing labels decide which feeds which: footer.explore.title
// is "Links column heading" and footer.visit.title is "Contact column heading",
// which are this design's two footer headings.
const linksTitle = String(f.explore?.title ?? '').trim();
const contactTitle = String(f.visit?.title ?? '').trim();

// ── THE LINKS COLUMN (D2). Three sources, two hook shapes, one index rule.
const exploreFromAdmin = Array.isArray(f.explore?.links) && f.explore.links.length > 0;
// content.navbar_links is what the sidebar list owns and what HeaderBO reads
// first; lay.navLinks is that same array passed through, or the builder's OWN
// four links when content carries none. Testing content tells us whether a hook
// would land on anything at all.
const navFromContent = Array.isArray(c.navbar_links) && c.navbar_links.length > 0;
type LinkRow = { label: string; href: string; i: number; labelHook?: string; hrefHook?: string };
const columnLinks: LinkRow[] = ((exploreFromAdmin
  ? f.explore.links.map((l: any) => ({ label: l?.text, href: l?.href }))
  : (navFromContent ? c.navbar_links : (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : []))) as any[])
  .map((l: any, i: number): LinkRow => ({
    label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(),
    href: onPage(l?.href, ''),
    i,
    // undefined when nothing owns the path — Astro omits an undefined
    // attribute, so those rows simply carry no hook.
    labelHook: exploreFromAdmin
      ? `footer.explore.links.${i}.text`
      : (navFromContent ? `navbar_links.${i}.label` : undefined),
    hrefHook: exploreFromAdmin
      ? `footer.explore.links.${i}.href`
      : (navFromContent ? `navbar_links.${i}.href` : undefined),
  }))
  .filter((l: LinkRow) => Boolean(l.label && l.href));

// ── THE CONTACT COLUMN. Two schema paths hold each of these facts, so the hook
// follows whichever one actually supplied the value (D5). content.location is
// passed through verbatim by the builder, so those two branches print exactly
// what is stored; content.contact has its phone display-formatted on the way
// through, which is why only that branch is marked derived.
const addrFromLocation = String(c.location?.address ?? '').trim();
const addrFromContact = String(c.contact?.address ?? lay.contact?.address ?? '').trim();
const address = addrFromLocation || addrFromContact;
const addressHook = addrFromLocation
  ? 'location.address'
  : (addrFromContact ? 'contact.address' : undefined);

const phoneFromLocation = String(c.location?.phone ?? '').trim();
const phoneFromContact = String(c.contact?.phone ?? lay.contact?.phone ?? '').trim();
const phone = phoneFromLocation || phoneFromContact;
const phoneHook = phoneFromLocation
  ? 'location.phone'
  : (phoneFromContact ? 'contact.phone' : undefined);
const phoneDerived = !phoneFromLocation && Boolean(phoneFromContact);
// Built, never stored (D6).
const phoneDigits = phone.replace(/[^0-9+]/g, '');

const email = String(c.contact?.email ?? lay.contact?.email ?? '').trim();

// The design's third contact row, in the owner's own words (D8).
type LineRow = { v: string; i: number };
const visitLines: LineRow[] = (Array.isArray(f.visit?.lines) ? f.visit.lines : [])
  .map((l: any, i: number): LineRow => ({ v: String(l ?? '').trim(), i }))
  .filter((l: LineRow) => Boolean(l.v));

// Colophon identity line: the design prints one line, and the address boxes are
// multi-line textareas. Flattened for THIS row only (hence data-derived on it).
const addressFlat = address.replace(/\s*\n+\s*/g, ', ');

const norm = (v: string): string => v.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim().toLowerCase();

// Colophon right cell (D1). Empty rows go, and so does a note that is an EXACT
// repeat of what the left cell already prints — nothing cleverer, because a ©
// line that happens to contain the name is different information from the
// name-and-address line beside it.
type NoteRow = { v: string; i: number };
const notes: NoteRow[] = (Array.isArray(f.notes) ? f.notes : [])
  .map((n: any, i: number): NoteRow => ({ v: String(n ?? '').trim(), i }))
  .filter((n: NoteRow) => Boolean(n.v) && norm(n.v) !== norm(brand) && norm(n.v) !== norm(addressFlat));

// ── GATES — BODY content only (R5). The two column headings never count: a
// heading is not a column, and one printed over nothing is the exact failure the
// rule exists for. footer.brand / footer.blurb / navbar_links / the header CTA /
// footer.notes ARE seeded by derive-content-defaults, but from the owner's own
// submitted name, city and business type — that is their data, not an invented
// headline.
const hasBrandCol = Boolean(brand || blurb || ctaText);
const hasContactCol = Boolean(address || phone || email || visitLines.length > 0);
const hasGrid = hasBrandCol || columnLinks.length > 0 || hasContactCol;
const hasColophon = Boolean(brand || addressFlat || notes.length > 0);
---
{(hasGrid || hasColophon) && (
<footer class="sip-foot">
  <div class="wrap">
    {hasGrid && (
      <div class:list={['sip-fgrid', hasColophon && 'sip-fgrid--ruled']}>
        {hasBrandCol && (
          <div class="sip-fbrand">
            {brand && (
              <div class="sip-flock">
                {/* Decoration over a name that is announced by the node beside
                    it, so it is hidden from the tree (R9). No hook: see the
                    DERIVED note in the frontmatter. */}
                {initial && <span class="sip-fseal" aria-hidden="true">{initial}</span>}
                <span class="sip-fmark" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>
              </div>
            )}
            {blurb && <p class="sip-fblurb" data-field="footer.blurb">{blurb}</p>}
            {/* Both hooks sit on the ANCHOR: the editor's link popover reads
                data-field from whatever carries data-href-field, so a label on
                an inner span would leave it editing an empty path. The arrow is
                generated content — editorBridge writes an inline edit back
                through innerHTML, so a glyph inside the element could end up
                stored in footer.cta.text; a pseudo-element cannot. */}
            {ctaText && (
              <a
                class="linkc sip-fcta"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="footer.cta.text"
                data-href-field="footer.cta.href"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}

        {columnLinks.length > 0 && (
          <div class="sip-fcol">
            {linksTitle && <h4 class="sip-fh" data-field="footer.explore.title">{linksTitle}</h4>}
            {columnLinks.map((l) => (
              <a
                class="sip-frow"
                href={l.href}
                data-field={l.labelHook}
                data-href-field={l.hrefHook}
              >{l.label}</a>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}

        {hasContactCol && (
          <div class="sip-fcol">
            {contactTitle && <h4 class="sip-fh" data-field="footer.visit.title">{contactTitle}</h4>}
            <address class="sip-faddr">
              {/* The stored string verbatim — `white-space: pre-line` draws the
                  owner's own line breaks, so the DOM text and the field value
                  stay identical and a live edit cannot lie. */}
              {address && (
                <span class="sip-frow sip-fline" data-field={addressHook}>{address}</span>
              )}
              {phone && (
                <a
                  class="sip-frow sip-fchan"
                  href={`tel:${phoneDigits}`}
                  data-field={phoneHook}
                  data-derived={phoneDerived ? '' : undefined}
                >{phone}</a>
              )}
              {/* mailto: is BUILT, never stored — the visible text stays the
                  plain address so an inline edit can never write "mailto:…"
                  back into the email box. */}
              {email && (
                <a class="sip-frow sip-fchan" href={`mailto:${email}`} data-field="contact.email">{email}</a>
              )}
              {visitLines.map((l) => (
                <span class="sip-frow sip-fline" data-field={`footer.visit.lines.${l.i}`}>{l.v}</span>
              ))}
            </address>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    )}

    {hasColophon && (
      <div class="sip-fcolo">
        {(brand || addressFlat) && (
          <span class="sip-fid">
            {brand && <span data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
            {brand && addressFlat && <span class="sip-fsep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>}
            {addressFlat && <span data-field={addressHook} data-derived>{addressFlat}</span>}
          </span>
        )}
        {notes.length > 0 && (
          <span class="sip-fnotes">
            {notes.map((n, d) => (
              <>
                {d > 0 && <span class="sip-fsep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>}
                <span data-field={`footer.notes.${n.i}`}>{n.v}</span>
              </>
            ))}
          </span>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</footer>
)}
<style>
  /* The design's closing page: bare --paper, 56px of air above the grid, 40px
     under the colophon, and NO top rule and no band (see D12 — the section
     above ends in an inset purple plate, and the page ground simply continues).
     The wrapper's `section` padding rule targets <section> only, so all of this
     padding is this file's.
     (Never write a literal end-style tag inside this block, comments included:
      it is an HTML raw-text element and the parser would end the stylesheet
      right there, silently truncating the file.) */
  .sip-foot {
    background-color: var(--paper);
    padding: 56px 0 40px;
  }

  /* 1.5fr / 1fr / 1fr at a 34px gap — the design's own tracks. minmax(0,…) on
     every one of them, also the design's, so a long unbroken address or link
     label cannot widen a column past its share and push the page sideways. */
  .sip-fgrid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 34px;
  }
  /* The hairline the design draws 32px under the grid — but it is the rule
     BETWEEN the grid and the colophon, so the markup adds this modifier only
     when a colophon follows. Left unconditional it becomes an empty rule 40px
     above the bottom of the page whenever brand, address and notes are all
     blank while a column still renders. --line is the hairline token; see the
     COLOUR note in the frontmatter for the one value in this footer that has no
     exact token. */
  .sip-fgrid--ruled {
    padding-bottom: 32px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .sip-fbrand, .sip-fcol { min-width: 0; }

  /* The lock: disc and wordmark centred on each other, 10px apart, 13px of
     space under them. Wraps rather than overflowing at 360px. */
  .sip-flock {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 13px; min-width: 0;
  }
  /* A circle, so it never shrinks — a squashed circle is an ellipse. Baloo 2 at
     the design's 400, and line-height is DECLARED: the design's body sets none,
     so its disc letter renders at the browser normal, while PageBO's body sets
     1.72 and would push a 20px glyph into a 34px line box inside a 36px well. */
  .sip-fseal {
    flex: none; width: 36px; height: 36px; border-radius: 50%;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    background-color: var(--accent); color: var(--on-accent);
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 20px; line-height: 1;
  }
  .sip-fmark {
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.1; color: var(--ink);
    min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Qualified by the parent class because it HAS to be (S1): the wrapper's
     paragraph reset is `html[data-page="BO"] :where(p) { margin: 0 0 1em }` at
     (0,1,1) and a bare `.sip-fblurb` scores (0,1,0) — the scoping attribute
     adds nothing — so unqualified this rule loses its margin outright. (0,2,0)
     wins it. 34ch, 1.78 and the 15px bottom margin are the design's own. */
  .sip-fbrand .sip-fblurb {
    margin: 0 0 15px;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.78;
    color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 34ch; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The design's 13.5px purple action. .linkc carries the family, the weight,
     the 44px tap floor and the underline mechanics, so only the size, the
     colour, the wrap rules and the arrow are added — the arrow as generated
     content, and with no margin: .linkc is an inline-flex box, so the
     pseudo-element is a flex ITEM and the primitive's own `gap: .5em` already
     spaces it. Both wrap declarations are needed: a label with no spaces in it
     needs overflow-wrap to break AND white-space to be allowed to wrap at all.
     QUALIFIED BY THE PARENT, and the colour RESTATED, for the reason in S1
     below: a bare `.sip-fcta` scores (0,1,0) and loses font-size to the
     wrapper's `:where(.linkc)` at (0,1,1) — the action would render at the
     primitive's 14px, not the design's 13.5px — while the colour it never
     declared falls to `html[data-page="BO"] a { color: inherit }` at (0,1,2),
     which out-specifies `:where(.linkc){color:var(--accent)}` and would print
     this purple action in --ink. (0,2,0) here clears both. */
  .sip-fbrand .sip-fcta {
    font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--accent);
    white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .sip-fbrand .sip-fcta::after { content: '\2192'; }
  /* The design's own a:hover, at (0,3,0) so it beats the rule above outright
     rather than tying with it and depending on source order. */
  .sip-fbrand .sip-fcta:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); border-bottom-color: var(--accent-deep); }

  /* Column headings: 10px purple caps at .24em with 13px under them — the
     design's own treatment, and note it carries NO underline. The eyebrow rule
     belongs to .kick and to the sections; these are restated rather than
     borrowed because the metrics differ (D4). Qualified by the parent for S1:
     a bare `.sip-fh` scores (0,1,0) and the wrapper's
     `html[data-page="BO"] :where(h1,h2,h3,h4)` scores (0,1,1), so an
     unqualified class loses family, weight, colour, tracking, uppercasing,
     line-height and margin all at once — the heading would render as black
     Baloo 2 in mixed case at -.01em with nothing under it. */
  .sip-fcol .sip-fh {
    margin: 0 0 13px;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 700; font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: .24em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    line-height: 1.4; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The design's rows: 14px --ink-soft on a ~27px pitch. Blocks rather than the
     design's paragraphs, so the whole row is the tap target and the last row
     leaves no margin dangling above the hairline (D13). */
  .sip-frow {
    display: block; padding: 3px 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 400; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    transition: color .18s ease;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* S1. THE ANCHOR ROWS NEED THE PARENT CLASS. Astro scopes a rule as
     `.sip-frow:where(.astro-<hash>)`, and :where() contributes NOTHING to
     specificity — so the block above scores (0,1,0) while the wrapper's
     `html[data-page="BO"] a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none }` scores
     (0,1,2) and wins. Unqualified, every link in these columns would drop
     --ink-soft for the inherited --ink while the plain address line beside it
     kept --ink-soft: two colours in one column. (0,2,1) clears it, and (0,3,1)
     on the hover so the hover beats the resting colour instead of tying. */
  .sip-fcol a.sip-frow { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .sip-fcol a.sip-frow:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .sip-frow { transition: none; } }

  .sip-faddr { font-style: normal; margin: 0; min-width: 0; }
  /* The stored string, unaltered — pre-line draws the owner's own line breaks
     without this file generating a single line-break tag. */
  .sip-fline { white-space: pre-line; }
  /* Phone and email are underlined; the static lines beside them are not (D9).
     text-decoration adds no box, so thickening it on hover cannot shift rows.
     Qualified for S1 too, and for the same rule: the wrapper's `a` sets
     `text-decoration: none` at (0,1,2), so an unqualified `.sip-fchan` at
     (0,1,0) would draw no underline at all and D9 would be a comment describing
     something the stylesheet never did. */
  .sip-fcol a.sip-fchan {
    text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: currentColor;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .sip-fcol a.sip-fchan:hover { text-decoration-color: var(--accent-deep); text-decoration-thickness: 2px; }

  /* The colophon: 20px under the hairline, two cells, space-between, 11px at
     weight 600 in --muted with no tracking and no uppercasing — the design's
     own row, which is why it is not .mono (D11). With one cell absent the
     remaining cell simply sits left. Wraps rather than overflows.
     line-height is DECLARED but not invented: the design sets none on this row,
     so it renders at the browser normal (~1.36 for Nunito Sans), while PageBO's
     body sets 1.72 — inheriting that would open the design's tightest row by
     half a line. 1.4 is the design's own leading, restated. */
  .sip-fcolo {
    padding-top: 20px;
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--muted); overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .sip-fid, .sip-fnotes { min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .sip-fsep { margin: 0 .45em; }

  /* ── RESPONSIVE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The wrapper owns the shared 760 / 520 pass; this file owns its own grid.
     Three columns cannot hold 14px rows and a 34ch blurb below ~900px, so they
     stack there — at ONE width, so there is never a band where a column is 90px
     wide. Every TYPE size and every enclosing gap below only ever shrinks as
     the viewport narrows, and there is no clamp() anywhere in this file to
     invert: nothing has a floor in one query sitting above a cap in the next.
     The two values that GROW are touch targets rather than type — at 900 a link
     row takes a 44px floor and a static line takes 9px of padding, which is
     where the columns stack and a row becomes a primary target — so nothing the
     reader is measuring gets bigger on a smaller screen. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .sip-fgrid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 28px; }
    .sip-fgrid--ruled { padding-bottom: 26px; }
    /* Stacked, each link row is a primary target rather than one of three
       columns. The static lines get air instead of a tap floor — they are not
       targets, and forcing a 44px box round a one-line address only opens a
       gap. */
    a.sip-frow { min-height: 44px; display: flex; align-items: center; }
    .sip-fline { padding: 9px 0; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .sip-foot { padding: 40px 0 28px; }
    .sip-fgrid { gap: 24px; }
    .sip-fgrid--ruled { padding-bottom: 22px; }
    .sip-fseal { width: 32px; height: 32px; font-size: 18px; }
    /* 24px x 1.1 = 26.4px becomes 21px x 1.1 = 23.1px: the line box shrinks
       with the type, not against it. */
    .sip-fmark { font-size: 21px; }
    .sip-fbrand .sip-fblurb { max-width: none; }
    /* Wide tracking on a full-width caps row is what actually pushes a 320px
       screen sideways, so the tracking comes in rather than the type. */
    .sip-fcol .sip-fh { letter-spacing: .18em; }
    .sip-fcolo { gap: 10px; }
    .sip-fsep { margin: 0 .3em; }
  }
</style>
